{
  "text": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E with its induced norm and Borel σ-algebra, every probability measure μ on E, and every real η > 0, let γ_E be the centered standard Gaussian probability measure with identity covariance and let J be the pushforward of μ ⊗ γ_E by (x,z) ↦ (x, x+√η·z). There exists a measurable Markov kernel R from E to E such that, for every y ∈ E, R(y) is exactly μ tilted by x ↦ −‖x−y‖²/(2η), and R is a conditional kernel for the swapped joint measure J_swap, the pushforward of J by (x,y) ↦ (y,x). Explicitly, define w_y(x)=exp(−‖x−y‖²/(2η)) and Z_y=∫_E w_y(x) dμ(x). The pointwise formula is R(y)=μ.withDensity(x ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(w_y(x)/Z_y)); its reference measure is μ, not Lebesgue measure. The denominator uses the prescribed totalized real integral, and no additional integrability premise is assumed. Markov means R(y) has total mass one for every y. Kernel measurability means y ↦ R(y,B) is measurable for each measurable output event B. If ν is the first marginal of J_swap, equivalently the second marginal of J, the conditional-kernel assertion is the equality of measures ν ⊗ₘ R = J_swap. On every measurable rectangle A×B this composition has mass given by the nonnegative integral ∫_A R(y,B) dν(y). Thus R provides a measurable version of the conditional law of the original first coordinate given the noisy second coordinate. The tilted formula is asserted for all y, including values in ν-null sets; no pointwise uniqueness among all conditional kernels or uniqueness of values on such null sets is asserted. The sole scale parameter is η>0, with the forward scaling √η and the exponent denominator exactly 2η. No density or moment assumption on μ is imposed. Dimension zero is allowed, using the unique standard Gaussian probability law there.",
